Block SIGHUP (and the other thread-handled signals) before any thread is created.

Upstream opentracker (2024-2026 master) spawns the UDP worker threads (listen.udp.workers N /
-p/-P options) while parsing the command line / config file, i.e. BEFORE main() calls
defaul_signal_handlers(). Those workers therefore inherit an UNBLOCKED SIGHUP; when systemd runs
`ExecReload=/bin/kill -HUP $MAINPID` the kernel may deliver the signal to a worker thread and the
whole process terminates (systemd logs "Deactivated successfully" because SIGHUP counts as a clean
exit) instead of re-reading the accesslist. Without listen.udp.workers the bug is invisible.

Apply on top of git://erdgeist.org/opentracker before `make`:
    cd opentracker && git apply sighup-udp-workers.patch

diff --git a/opentracker.c b/opentracker.c
index f63db72..871291f 100644
--- a/opentracker.c
+++ b/opentracker.c
@@ -655,6 +655,11 @@ int main(int argc, char **argv) {
   char     *statefile = 0;
   pthread_t thread_id; /* time cacher */
 
+  /* Block the signals handled by dedicated threads (SIGHUP → accesslist reload via sigwait) BEFORE any
+     thread is created: UDP worker threads are spawned while parsing -p/-P/listen.* options and would
+     otherwise inherit an unblocked SIGHUP and terminate the process on `systemctl reload`. */
+  defaul_signal_handlers();
+
   memset(serverip, 0, sizeof(ot_ip6));
 #ifdef WANT_V4_ONLY
   serverip[10] = serverip[11] = -1;
